WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 61 for June 4th, 2026.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:11.120
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it. Let's go.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.580
So imagine you could carry a powerful Swiss Army knife in your pocket instead of hauling an entire

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:23.460
toolbox everywhere. That is basically what Google just did with a new open-source model called

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:30.720
Gemma 412b. It is a single AI system that handles text, images, and audio altogether.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:37.280
And the really big part is that it runs smoothly on ordinary laptops with just 16 gigabytes of ram.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.820
Think of it like fitting a full orchestra into a backpack instead of needing a concert hall.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.880
Most strong AI tools still sit in the cloud and need an internet connection plus a subscription.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.180
This one works right on your own device so your chats and pictures stay private with no monthly bill.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:01:01.140
For students doing creative projects or anyone curious about AI, it opens the door to real

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:07.400
experiments without limits or costs. You can already download it and try it on a regular laptop.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:13.380
It nearly matches much larger models on many tasks while staying light enough for everyday hardware.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:19.640
If you have a laptop with 16 gigabytes of ram, this is one of the first times a truly capable

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:25.180
multimodal model has been this accessible. Okay now for my favorite part of the show,

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:31.580
the deep dive where we look under the hood. Imagine you are watching a video with captions and narration

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:37.460
at the same time. Your brain connects the pictures, the spoken words, and the text on screen without

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:44.800
thinking about it. That is what multimodal AI tries to copy. First the model turns every kind of input

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.480
into the same kind of number patterns inside its system. Pixels from a photo become numbers,

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:57.720
letters from text become numbers, and sound waves become numbers too. Then it looks for connections

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.720
across all those numbers at once. When you give it a homework screenshot plus a voice note about

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:10.020
what you are stuck on, it combines both clues the way you would. And that is basically how multimodal

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:16.420
AI works. Not so scary, right? Now let's move to cool stuff and try this, starting with a playful

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:22.420
Google tool. Dream beans pulls photos, locations, and calendar events from your Google account.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.580
It turns them into illustrated story pages that look like a cartoon version of your own life.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.580
Think of it like turning your weekend photos into pages of a comic book that tells the story for you.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:39.240
Anyone with a Google account can explore it, though younger listeners may need a parent to sign in.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:44.880
Go to the Google app or search for dream beans in your browser, connect your account,

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.980
and pick a recent trip or school event. Watch how it weaves your photos into a short,

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:55.760
illustrated story with captions. It is a fun way to see your memories turn into something creative

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.460
without any drawing skills. Next up is a handy feature inside the Amazon shopping app.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:04.920
You can type a description like Blue Hoodie with a small pocket,

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:11.700
and it shows AI-generated pictures of items that match. Think of it like sketching an idea in words

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:16.800
and having the app paint possible versions for you to browse. You tap the picture you like,

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:22.560
and it searches for real similar products you can buy. Open the Amazon shopping app on your phone,

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:27.900
tap the search bar, describe something you are looking for, and scroll through the generated images.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:33.320
It is a quick way to explore ideas when you know what you want, but not the exact name.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.160
Now for quick bits, two fast stories worth knowing.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:44.260
Amazon's latest warehouse robot called Proteus can take spoken instructions in normal everyday language.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:49.600
Workers no longer need to write special code or use technical commands to work with it.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.060
This makes it easier for people without technical training to team up with the machines.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:04:01.440
In other news, a British member of Parliament is taking XAI to court over its GROC image generator.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.180
The case asks whether the company should be legally responsible when his AI creates images

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:13.920
that could harm real people. It is one of the first big legal tests of accountability for AI-generated

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:20.780
pictures. And that is a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool. Go play with it.

